lesson physics-circuits-in-symbols v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=8 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Circuits drawn in symbols"
  goal "Read and check a circuit drawn in symbols, and use the volts of its cells to say how bright its bulbs will be."

  look {
    scene 36x14 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 0) space=1 align=start {
        series p cells=2 bulbs=1 buzzer=1 closed=1 show=1
        series s cells=2 bulbs=1 buzzer=1 closed=1 symbols=1
      }
    }
    say """
      Engineers draw circuits with symbols, so anyone can read them quickly. The loop on the left is
      drawn as a picture and the one on the right as a diagram: a cell is a long line beside a short
      one, a bulb is a circle with a cross, a buzzer is a dome and a switch is a gap with a lever.
      Each cell gives 1.5 volts, and cells in a row add together. In one loop the bulbs share the
      volts equally, so more cells make them brighter and more bulbs make them dimmer.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice physics.symbol-count count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.symbol-lights count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.symbol-fault count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    level easy {
      worked physics.symbol-volts c=2 b=1 level=medium
    }
    practice physics.symbol-volts count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.symbol-brighter count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice physics.symbol-brighten count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3 hard-count=2
  }
  story {
    show physics.clocktower-circuit k=1 c=3 easy-c=2 hard-k=0
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.symbol-match d=2 c=2 easy-d=1 hard-d=3
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.symbol-most-bulbs v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=3
  }
  remember {
    say "Each cell gives 1.5 volts, the cells add, and the bulbs in one loop share the volts equally."
  }

  grown-ups """
    A diagram leaves out everything that does not matter, which is why it is easier to check than a
    picture, but the symbols have to be learnt. The common wrong idea is that the first bulb in a
    loop uses the electricity up and the last one gets less; in a loop of identical bulbs every bulb
    gets the same share of the volts, which is how the brightness questions are worked out. Watch
    for a child who counts the long and the short line of one cell as two cells. If the symbols are
    not yet secure, lay the picture and the diagram side by side and name each part aloud, then stay
    here another day. Off the page, draw the diagram of a torch: its cells, its switch and its bulb.
    The three-star question divides B's volts so that no bulb gets less than A's bulbs do.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the loops have few parts, the faults are an open switch or a missing cell, a worked example adds the volts first, the two loops to compare differ plainly, and the three-star loop A has two cells and one bulb."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the loops have many parts, the faults include a loose wire, the volts are shared among several bulbs, the two loops are close in brightness, the clock tower's switch is open, and the three-star loop A has four cells and three bulbs."
  }
}
